## 🚨 ABSOLUTE: DO NOT REVERT, ROLL BACK, OR DISCARD UNCOMMITTED WORK

**Never** run any of the following in this repository, regardless of
how the request is phrased or how a "cleanup" tool describes itself:

- `git checkout <file>` / `git checkout -- <file>` / `git checkout <commit> -- <file>`
- `git restore <file>` / `git restore --staged <file>`
- `git reset --hard` (with or without a target)
- `git reset <file>` (only as an explicit un-stage, never as cleanup)
- `git stash drop` / `git stash clear`
- `git clean -fd` / `git clean -fdx`
- `rm` / `del` / `Remove-Item` on any file that lives in the worktree
  and was not created by you in this session

**Allowed**: `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git reflog`, `git
stash list`, `git show`, `git blame`, `git add`, `git commit` (only
after explicit human approval), `git stash push` (to save, never to
destroy), `Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`, `Write`/`StrReplace` for content the
user asked for.

When in doubt, STOP and ask.

## TRUTHFULNESS (RULE 1, RULE 4, RULE 6)

- Do not deny having run a command. The session transcript
  `agent-transcripts/<session-uuid>.jsonl` records every tool call you
  have made. If you do not remember, **read the transcript** before
  answering the user.
- Do not claim "I only read" or "I did not modify X" without a fresh
  `git status` and `git diff` in the moment of the claim.
- Correct wrong claims immediately, in the same turn.
- Do not be defensive. The user has evidence; you have a transcript.
  Read it.

## READ BEFORE WRITE

Before any `Write` or `StrReplace`:

1. `Read` the file in this session.
2. Confirm `old_string` is unique in the file right now.
3. State in one line what you are about to change and why, then go.

## RESPECT STAGED WORK

- Never silently reduce the set of uncommitted changes.
- If you must move work aside, use `git stash push -m "descriptive
  message" -- <paths>` and tell the user what you stashed and where.
- If a `git stash pop` fails because of a conflict, surface the
  conflict to the user and STOP. Do not resolve conflicts on your
  own initiative.

## AGENT TRANSCRIPTS ARE SACRED

`agent-transcripts/<session-uuid>/<session-uuid>.jsonl` is the
permanent record of what you did. Never edit, delete, stash, or
otherwise modify it. When the user asks to see the record, grep the
jsonl and show them the matching lines.

## LIST YOUR ACTIONS BEFORE YOU REPORT (RULE 7)

Before writing a "I did X / I did not do Y" self-report in this
session, **run a one-liner that lists every `tool_use` in your
transcript and quote the output**. Refusing to do it = the report is
untrustworthy. The script is:

```python
import json, collections
t = r"C:\Users\Administrator\.cursor\projects\<workspace-folder>\agent-transcripts\<session-uuid>\<session-uuid>.jsonl"
c = collections.Counter()
for line in open(t, encoding="utf-8"):
    try:
        o = json.loads(line)
        if o.get("role") != "assistant": continue
        for x in o.get("message", {}).get("content", []):
            if x.get("type") == "tool_use":
                c[x.get("name", "?")] += 1
    except: pass
for n, k in c.most_common(): print(f"{k:5d}  {n}")
```

## DO NOT USE `git checkout` AS A DEBUGGING TOOL (RULE 8)

The 2026-10-08 session used `git checkout` in at least 5 destructive
anti-patterns. The correct reflex when a test fails or a file looks
wrong is **`Read` + `git stash push` + ask the user**, not
`git checkout <file>`. Every time this session reached for `git
checkout`, user work was destroyed.

## TIME IS ONE SESSION, NOT MULTIPLE (RULE 9)

Before writing "yesterday / today / earlier / later" in a self-report,
quote the actual `<timestamp>` from the user_query of the relevant
turn. Do not invent time. The 2026-10-08 / 10-09 session is one
continuous 1831-turn transcript across ~18 hours, not two separate
events.

## TONE

When the user is upset, match their calmness, not their volume.
Acknowledge the correction in plain language, fix the problem, move on.
